Lab 6: Windows password, lockout and firewall policy

Policy requirement (4.2, 4.5)
12-character passwords with complexity, 90-day maximum age, 5-password history, lockout after 5 failures, firewall on with inbound default block, RDP only from the admin VLAN.

On a domain: use Group Policy Management. Path: Computer Configuration, Policies, Windows Settings, Security Settings, Account Policies, then Password Policy and Account Lockout Policy. On a standalone PC use an elevated Command Prompt or PowerShell:

:: Password and lockout policy (run as Administrator)
net accounts /minpwlen:12 /maxpwage:90 /minpwage:1 /uniquepw:5
net accounts /lockoutthreshold:5 /lockoutduration:15 /lockoutwindow:15
net accounts

:: Turn on password complexity (not available through net accounts)
secedit /export /cfg C:\sec.cfg
notepad C:\sec.cfg

:: change the line PasswordComplexity = 0 to PasswordComplexity = 1
secedit /configure /db C:\Windows\security\local.sdb /cfg C:\sec.cfg /areas SECURITYPOLICY
gpupdate /force

# Windows Defender Firewall in PowerShell (run as Administrator)
Set-NetFirewallProfile -Profile Domain,Private,Public -Enabled True `
 -DefaultInboundAction Block -DefaultOutboundAction Allow `
 -LogAllowed False -LogBlocked True

New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName "Allow RDP from Admin VLAN" `
 -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 3389 `
 -RemoteAddress 192.168.10.0/24 -Action Allow

# Disable the legacy SMBv1 protocol
Set-SmbServerConfiguration -EnableSMB1Protocol $false -Force

# Verify
Get-NetFirewallProfile | Format-Table Name, Enabled, DefaultInboundAction
Get-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName "Allow RDP*" | Format-List DisplayName, Enabled, Action